    not_duplicate_of: []  # f2f6
    pr_ref:  # f2f6
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-26 while auditing std::fs for WI-bupor, and it BLOCKS that audit.\n\nTHE BLOCKER, stated first because it is the practical consequence. std::fs::File\nis catalogued today with three ASSOCIATED FUNCTIONS (open, create, create_new)\nand NOT ONE of its ~12 stable I/O METHODS (metadata, set_len, set_permissions,\nset_times, sync_all, sync_data, lock, lock_shared, try_lock, try_lock_shared,\nunlock). Adding those methods -- which is simply the correct catalogue content\nand is exactly what WI-bupor exists to do -- REGRESSES every Rust repo that\nopens a file without calling a File method. Measured live:\n\n    std::fs::File kinds TODAY: {'function'}          starved: []\n    after adding File::metadata as a method:          starved: ['std::fs::File']\n\nONE CORRECT ROW FLIPS A CLEAN VERDICT TO A WITHHELD ONE. So the predicate\nPENALISES CORRECT CATALOGUING, and the incentive it creates is to\nUNDER-catalogue -- to leave real I/O out so the module stays function-only.\nThat is the opposite of what the stdlib audit campaign is for.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. method_starved_modules (verify_claims.py:2953) computes\nmethod_modules as every module carrying ANY method-kind primitive, then for each\ncall edge marks the module `called`, and marks it `satisfied` ONLY when that\nedge's meta.call_construct == \"method\". It returns called - satisfied. The\ngranularity is THE MODULE, but the property it tests is PER-KIND. A module\ncarrying both kinds can never be satisfied by a function-construct call, even\nwhen the catalogue matched that call perfectly.\n\nThe docstring states the intent exactly, and the code does not implement it:\n\"when a repo calls into a method-keyed module and the analyzer produced no\nmethod-construct edge for it, the catalogue was never given anything it could\nmatch -- the analysis did not look.\" For a MIXED module the catalogue WAS given\nsomething it could match. The analysis did look.\n\nIT IS ALREADY FIRING, AND TODAY'S OWN FIX CREATED THREE OF THE CASES. PR #552\n(INV-nular) split rust's miskinded associated functions out of `methods:` into\n`functions:`. That fixed the MATCH but not the STARVATION, because the modules\nnow carry both kinds. Measured on the shipped catalogue with a\nfunction-construct call edge:\n\n    std::net::TcpStream    kinds=['function','method']  starved=YES  (connect)\n    std::net::UdpSocket    kinds=['function','method']  starved=YES  (bind)\n    std::process::Command  kinds=['function','method']  starved=YES  (new)\n    std::fs::File          kinds=['function']           starved=no   (open)\n\nstd::fs::File is clean ONLY because it is under-catalogued. The three that were\ncorrected today are the three that now starve. PR #552's closure note should be\nread with this correction: it fixed classification, not coverage.\n\nEIGHT MIXED-KIND MODULES SHIP TODAY across all catalogues -- rust 4 (above),\njavascript 2 (Deno, fs), python 1 (pathlib.Path), scala 1 (scala.io.Source).\npathlib.Path is the split made in the same PR #552, so python has the same\nexposure.\n\nPROPOSED FIX, not decided here. Evaluate starvation PER KIND rather than per\nmodule: a call edge should mark its module satisfied when the edge's construct\nmatches ANY kind the module declares, so a function-construct call into a module\nwith function-kind entries counts as \"the catalogue was given something it could\nmatch\". A module is then starved only when every call into it was of a construct\nthe catalogue cannot match -- which is the docstring's own stated intent.\n\nDIRECTION OF RISK, flagged because it decides the evidence bar: this is a\nGATE-LOOSENING change. Fewer starved modules means MORE clean verdicts, which is\nthe false-all-clear direction. The standing owner rule is that loosening needs\nits own cross-language measurement and never rides along with a fix pointing the\nother way. So this must NOT be bundled into WI-bupor's catalogue PR even though\nit is what unblocks it."  # f2f6
    fields:  # f2f6
      statement: "A module is method-starved only when every call into it used a construct the catalogue cannot match. A module declaring both function-kind and method-kind primitives must be satisfied by a function-construct call that matches its function-kind entries, and is not starved merely because no method-construct edge landed."  # f2f6
      root_cause: "method_starved_modules builds method_modules as every module carrying ANY method-kind primitive, then marks a module satisfied only when an edge's meta.call_construct == 'method'. The granularity is the module; the property is per-kind. Its own docstring states the intent the code does not implement."  # f2f6
